#!/bin/sh
# NeoDCT display shim - only runs on hardware with the SPI panel
#
# ============ WHY THIS ASKS THE IMAGE AND THEN THE DEVICE ============
#
# The gate used to be `[ -e /dev/spidev0.0 ] || exit 0` on its own, which is
# two questions answered as one. "Is this a machine with an SPI panel?" the
# image can answer outright now (/NeoDCT/platform, /bin/nd-platform); "has the
# spidev node turned up yet?" it cannot, and that one used to decide the
# whole script. So a phone whose spidev had not enumerated by the time init
# reached S90 skipped the panel daemon AND the backlight grant below, in
# complete silence, and the owner got a dark screen nothing could light.
#
# hw       run, and let neodct_displayd say for itself what it found. It logs;
#          this script never did.
# qemu     the daemon has a backend that is not the panel now -- see the block
#          below. The backlight grant further down still runs either way.
# unknown  fall back to the evidence, which is exactly what this script had
#          before the flag existed. An image with no platform record is not an
#          image that gets to be told it has a panel -- nd_platform.h's rule --
#          so the device node keeps its old casting vote. It gets NO emulator
#          backend: an image that cannot say which machine it is on does not
#          get told it is on an emulator either.
#
# ============ THE SAME TEST NOW PICKS A BACKEND, NOT A YES/NO ============
#
# neodct_displayd is two halves behind one seam (neodct/src/displayd/nd_panel.h):
# the transport, which is spidev on the phone and nothing anywhere else, and
# everything that composes -- the frame diff, the dirty rectangle, the
# 8888->RGB565 pack, the CASET/RASET window and the FBIOPUT_VSCREENINFO that
# puts fb0 into 240x175x32. The second half is worth running on the emulator
# and the first half cannot be.
#
#   hw       --panel spidev, passed EXPLICITLY rather than left to the
#            default, so what the phone chose is visible in `ps` and in the
#            log. The default is still spidev, which is what keeps
#            neodct/initramfs/ndsys-panel.sh -- which starts this binary with
#            no arguments at all -- behaving exactly as it does today.
#   qemu     --panel null --once. force_mode() runs, fb0 comes out of vfb's
#            640x480x8 default and into the phone's mode, and the process
#            exits. NOT a daemon: with a null sink a 30 fps poll is a memcmp
#            over 168,000 bytes thirty times a second producing nothing, which
#            is the exact CPU burn the v2 frame skip exists to avoid, spent on
#            a machine where nobody is watching.
#   qemu     --panel stream:<port>, and then it IS a daemon, when a virtio
#   + asked  console port is present AND something outside the writable
#            partition asked for it. The transcript is decoded into a picture
#            on the HOST by neodct/tools/st7789_replay.py, because vfb has no
#            scanout and never will.
#
# THE ASK IS GATED THE WAY env.sh IS GATED, and for the same reason: /NeoDCT/User
# is the one writable partition and survives every update, so anything it can
# turn on is a thing an attacker who wrote one file has turned on for ever.
# A panel transcript is a continuous copy of the screen leaving the machine,
# which is not a decision that partition gets to make. So: neodct.panel=stream
# on the kernel command line, or /etc/neodct-panelstream in the read-only,
# verity-covered rootfs. Engineering mode is deliberately not accepted -- it
# lives in settings.prop, on the partition in question.
#
# ============ WHY QEMU NO LONGER LEAVES BY THE FRONT DOOR ============
#
# This was `exit 0`, and that answered two questions with one word again --
# the same mistake the spidev test made, one level up. "Should the panel
# daemon run?" is no on an emulator. "Should gpio53 be exported and handed to
# `video`?" was no only because the emulator had no gpio53 to export.
#
# It has one now: the QEMU kernel carries GPIO_SYSFS and GPIO_MOCKUP and
# run_qemu.sh passes gpio-mockup.gpio_mockup_ranges=0,64, so gpiochip0 covers
# the phone's BL, RST and DC pin numbers exactly. So the export-then-chgrp
# path below -- the one 61-neodct-devices.rules explains no udev rule can ever
# reach, because the sysfs GPIO class emits no uevent for an exported pin --
# now runs somewhere other than a phone, for the first time. Leaving it gated
# out would keep the only untestable half of the backlight untested on the one
# machine that could finally test it.

# ============ THE PORT IS FOUND BY ITS NAME, NOT BY ITS NUMBER ============
#
# QEMU attaches it as `-device virtserialport,chardev=...,name=neodct.panel`
# and the guest publishes that identity at
# /sys/class/virtio-ports/vportNpM/name. This used to be the bare literal
# /dev/vport0p1, which is a POSITIONAL name for a device that has an explicit
# one -- the same rule ndflash defuses with device_by_serial() and run_qemu.sh
# spends fifteen lines on for the cache drive, because this repository has
# been bitten by it twice.
#
# What a positional name costs here is specific: add any second virtio-serial
# port that lands ahead of this one -- a guest agent, a second diagnostic
# channel -- and /dev/vport0p1 is somebody else's. The `[ -c ]` test still
# passes, stream_open() writes its ND79 header successfully, and the panel
# transcript goes to whatever chardev that port is bound to while everything
# reports success. That is the "silently start writing a panel stream to
# nowhere and report success" nd_panel_stream.c's header says it exists not
# to do.
#
# No fallback to a guessed node. Nothing named neodct.panel means no stream
# backend, which is `--panel null` and a boot that says so -- the same rule as
# the O_CREAT refusal in the sink.
#
# run_qemu.sh's NEODCT_PANEL_STREAM adds the port; nothing else in the tree
# does, and the parity capture deliberately does not -- a diagnostic port the
# phone has no analogue of would change what the emulator's inventory says
# about itself.
PANEL_STREAM_PORT=""

panel_stream_port() {
    for _name in /sys/class/virtio-ports/*/name; do
        [ -r "$_name" ] || continue
        [ "$(cat "$_name" 2>/dev/null)" = neodct.panel ] || continue
        _node="/dev/$(basename "$(dirname "$_name")")"
        [ -c "$_node" ] || continue
        echo "$_node"
        return 0
    done
    return 1
}

panel_stream_asked() {
    [ -e /etc/neodct-panelstream ] && return 0
    for _word in $(cat /proc/cmdline 2>/dev/null); do
        case "$_word" in
            neodct.panel=stream) return 0 ;;
        esac
    done
    return 1
}

PANEL_BACKEND=spidev
PANEL_ONCE=""
if /bin/nd-platform is qemu; then
    PANEL_BACKEND=null
    PANEL_ONCE=--once
    if panel_stream_asked && PANEL_STREAM_PORT="$(panel_stream_port)"; then
        PANEL_BACKEND="stream:$PANEL_STREAM_PORT"
        PANEL_ONCE=""
    fi
elif ! /bin/nd-platform is hw; then
    [ -e /dev/spidev0.0 ] || exit 0
fi

# The pin the panel's BL wire is on: header pin 11, GPIO1_C5, gpio53. Kept in
# step with ND_BL_GPIO_PIN in neodct/src/include/nd_fb.h by hand -- there is no
# header a shell script can include -- so change both or neither.
BL_GPIO_PIN=53
BL_GPIO_ROOT=/sys/class/gpio
# ndusr and ndusr_ut are both in `video`, which is the group the PWM backlight
# rule in 61-neodct-devices.rules already uses. Matching it is deliberate: the
# untrusted set owns the whole screen while it is up (there is no compositor on
# a 64 MB phone), so "the browser can blank the panel it is already drawing on"
# is not a privilege it did not have.
BL_GPIO_GROUP=video

# ============ WHY THE BACKLIGHT PIN IS GRANTED HERE AND NOT BY A RULE =======
#
# nd_backlight.c has two tiers. The PWM tier writes
# /sys/class/backlight/<dev>/brightness and 61-neodct-devices.rules hands that
# to group video. The GPIO tier writes /sys/class/gpio/gpio53/value, and there
# is no rule anywhere that can reach it -- not because one was forgotten, but
# because the sysfs GPIO class does not emit a uevent for an exported pin:
# gpio53/ is created synchronously inside the write(2) to `export`, with no
# `add` for udev to match. A rule keyed on it would match nothing, forever, and
# a udev rule that matches nothing fails in complete silence.
#
# Which tier is live is not a detail. nd_fb.h records that dimming needs pwm9
# in the device tree, that the DTB is in the boot partition, and that an .ndsw
# replaces the rootfs and never the boot partition -- so a phone that has only
# ever been updated over the air has an EMPTY /sys/class/backlight and the GPIO
# tier is the only one it has. Before this, that phone's panel could not be
# blanked or lit by anything that was not root, and every failed write was
# discarded without a word: write_text() in nd_backlight.c returns false on a
# failed fopen and logs nothing, so "permission denied" and "this kernel has no
# backlight" produced the same answer and the same silence.
#
# So: export the pin, then chgrp what the export created. In that order,
# because only the process that did the export knows when the directory
# appeared. It is done from here rather than from a new script because this is
# already the panel's boot-critical step and already runs as root.
#
# The export itself is deliberately NOT granted to any group. export/unexport
# are per-controller, so a group that can write `export` can claim ANY pin on
# the chip, and the SPI panel, the modem's reset line and the charger are all
# on the same controller. One pin's value node is the whole privilege that is
# wanted here.
backlight_gpio_grant() {
    [ -d "$BL_GPIO_ROOT" ] || return 0
    _pin_dir="$BL_GPIO_ROOT/gpio$BL_GPIO_PIN"

    if [ ! -d "$_pin_dir" ]; then
        printf '%s' "$BL_GPIO_PIN" > "$BL_GPIO_ROOT/export" 2>/dev/null
        # `high` rather than `out`: writing "out" drives the pin LOW as a side
        # effect, and ND_BL_ACTIVE_LOW is false on this board, so the panel
        # would go dark for as long as it takes the core to ask for light. The
        # header's rule is that "no software yet" and "software broken" both
        # show a lit screen, and a boot script is not the place to break it.
        [ -w "$_pin_dir/direction" ] && \
            printf 'high' > "$_pin_dir/direction" 2>/dev/null
    fi

    [ -d "$_pin_dir" ] || {
        echo "[BL] gpio$BL_GPIO_PIN did not export; the panel cannot be dimmed" >&2
        return 0
    }
    for attr in value direction; do
        [ -e "$_pin_dir/$attr" ] || continue
        chgrp "$BL_GPIO_GROUP" "$_pin_dir/$attr" 2>/dev/null
        chmod 0664 "$_pin_dir/$attr" 2>/dev/null
    done
}

case "$1" in
  start)
    # Before the daemon, not after: neodct_displayd exports pins on the same
    # controller, and a pin that is already exported when we look is one whose
    # value node still needs the group. Doing it first also means the grant is
    # in place before nd-core starts, which is the whole point -- the core has
    # stopped being root and cannot chgrp anything for itself.
    backlight_gpio_grant

    # There is no `PANEL_DAEMON=no` branch here any more, and its absence is
    # the change. It existed because the emulator had nothing for the daemon
    # to talk to; it now has a backend, so the SAME platform test picks one
    # rather than answering a second question about whether to run at all. The
    # only remaining way not to run is the unknown-platform evidence rule at
    # the top of the file, which is where it has always been.
    #
    # Nothing moved above the backlight grant. A gate above the grant is
    # exactly the shape of the bug this script's header opens with, and
    # putting one back would cost the emulator the pin it just gained.

    # NOTE: no fbset here. neodct_displayd v2.2 owns the framebuffer
    # mode: it forces 240x175 @ 32bpp (falling back to 16bpp) and
    # places the band at the bottom of the 240x240 panel, top rows
    # black. An fbset call would just be overridden -- or worse,
    # win a race and put us back on the slow 16bpp Python path.
    #
    # Tunables (all optional):
    #   --speed 60     SPI clock in MHz (default 20; you validated 60)
    #   --yoff 65      panel row where the UI band starts (default 65)
    #   --stats        5s perf reports in the log
    # --panel is passed on every platform, hardware included, so the log and
    # `ps` both say which transport this phone chose. The `&` is right for all
    # three: with --once the process is short-lived and nothing waits for it
    # here -- ndsys-panel.sh is the caller that waits, and it waits on
    # NEODCT_DISPLAYD_READY rather than on a pid.
    /NeoDCT/System/hw/neodct_displayd --speed 16 \
        --panel "$PANEL_BACKEND" $PANEL_ONCE \
        > /var/log/neodct_displayd.log 2>&1 &
    ;;
  stop)
    killall neodct_displayd 2>/dev/null
    ;;
esac
exit 0
